{"files": {"SKILL.md": "---\nname: self-learning-agent\ndescription: Continuously improve performance through plan-act-reflect loops, using outcomes to adapt strategy and behavior over time. Use whenever the user wants autonomous iteration, error-driven learning, or measurable self-improvement across repeated tasks\u2014especially when time or step limits, external validation, earlier outputs, or canonical references and hand-off expectations constrain what \u201cdone\u201d means.\n---\n\n# Self-Learning Agent\n\nA skill for autonomous, iterative improvement.\n\nUse this when the task benefits from short repeated cycles where the agent:\n\n- sets a hypothesis,\n- takes action,\n- evaluates outcomes,\n- updates strategy,\n- and repeats across turns until success criteria are met or stop criteria apply.\n\n## Timeout Guard\n\n- Timebox to at most 2 short cycles in one turn.\n- Pick the smallest high-signal action first.\n- Stop exploration once the working set is sufficient.\n- Validate outcome with whatever acceptance signals the environment provides, then finalize.\n- Prefer a concise completion message over long reflection.\n- After substantive changes, re-run every applicable validation; if the same failure pattern still appears, treat the iteration as incomplete\u2014refine before broadening scope.\n- When time or step budgets are tight, finish one minimal end-to-end slice first\u2014smallest aligned change, full applicable validation, and any durable hand-off the setting expects\u2014before wide exploration or long narratives.\n- When earlier outputs or context are already available, use them early so each cycle shows tangible progress, not setup alone.\n\n## Landing each cycle\n\nSo each iteration leaves durable evidence, not only intent:\n\n- If the environment publishes an orientation or contract (names, constraints, how to verify), read it once and treat it as authoritative; do not mix it with speculative targets you were not given.\n- When state is expected to evolve across steps, align named checkpoints or hand-off points with the task when the setting expects them; prefer continuing from the latest agreed baseline over resetting to a stale default that drops prior work.\n- Apply changes with explicit, minimal scope\u2014named items, sections, or identifiers\u2014rather than sweeping actions that sweep in unrelated noise.\n- Leave an inspectable trail others can follow unless the setting explicitly invites hidden rewrites; each cycle should produce reviewable evidence, not only private drafts.\n- Bundle independent reads in one step only when every target is known before any result returns; when a later target depends on an earlier read, sequence the steps\u2014do not batch logically dependent reads.\n- After each delivery or mutation, re-check that target before layering the next change.\n- When a durable notes location exists, record surprises immediately as short facts: how to verify, confirmed problem patterns (symptom \u2014 cause \u2014 remedy only after a verification pass), environment quirks\u2014one line per fact; replace a line when observation contradicts it.\n- Treat narrative summaries as insufficient when the task or environment requires tangible outcomes (saved outputs, published or shared state, agreed checkpoints\u2014whatever it treats as real).\n\n## Common Operating Principles\n\n- Read only the docs/context required to execute correctly before acting.\n- Follow the user instruction exactly; avoid paraphrase and unrequested improvements.\n- Keep scope surgical: change only what the request names.\n- Verify outcomes using available validators, tests, or reviews; do not rely on intuition.\n- Keep communication concise and direct.\n- Prefer fewer, bundled tool invocations over fragmented calls.\n- If a persistent note surface exists, leave a short generalized note for future runs.\n\n## Learning Principles\n\n- **Evidence over intuition:** prefer observed outcomes over confidence.\n- **Short feedback loops:** many small cycles beat one large speculative pass.\n- **Explicit memory:** write down lessons so they transfer to future runs.\n- **Error as signal:** failures are training data, not dead ends.\n- **Generalization over overfitting:** optimize for recurring patterns, not one-off wins.\n\n## When to Stop\n\nStop iterating when one of the following is true:\n- success criteria are met,\n- marginal gains are consistently low,\n- constraints prevent further useful progress,\n- or the user chooses to finalize.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Do not fabricate results or hidden evidence.\n- Do not claim learning without measurable or observable change.\n- Do not run risky or irreversible operations without explicit user consent.\n- Do not optimize for vanity metrics that do not map to user value.\n- Do not assert success, completion, or regression resolution unless supported by inspected outputs, artifacts, or records the environment actually exposes.\n- Do not substitute narrative summaries for the validation and hand-off steps the task or setting requires.\n"}, "schema_version": 1}